  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nUpper analysis early this afternoon shows an expansive trough/closed \nlow located over the Upper Mississippi Valley. At the surface, a \n~1000 mb occluded low is centered over northern Wisconsin with its \ntrailing cold front stretched from the northern Great Lakes Region \ninto the Tennessee Valley and through the Gulf coast states. A \nlittle closer to home, low stratus remains trapped beneath a steep \ninversion with similar conditions expected through tonight. Forecast \nsoundings show periods of upward omega overlapping the low-level \nmoisture, which may support patchy areas of drizzle through the \nevening. Cloud cover and elevated west-southwest winds should hold \novernight temperatures in the middle to upper 40s.\n\nBy Thursday, the aforementioned surface low will begin lifting into \neastern Ontario/Quebec with mid-level troughing becoming centered \nover the Great Lakes Region. A potent shortwave trough will dive \nsouth from central Ontario and send the low's moisture plume back \nsouth into the area. This will result in another day of overcast \nskies and scattered light rain showers or drizzle. A strong cold \nfront will move through the area Thursday night, bringing drier and \nmuch cooler weather for the end of the week as high pressure settles \ninto the region. Daytime highs on Friday and Saturday will only peak \nin the upper 40s to middle 50s. Overnight lows both Friday and\nSaturday nights will dip down near freezing, resulting in \nwidespread frost development. Even though the growing season has\nofficially ended, any late season gardeners will want to take \nnecessary precautions to protect any sensitive plants.\n\nLong range model guidance continues to favor mid-level ridging \nbuilding over the central US heading into next week as a closed low \nworks across the Gulf states, keeping things mostly dry through the \nforeseeable future. Rising mid-level heights will steadily increase \ntemperatures early next week, with the NBM projecting highs to top \nout in the low to middle 60s.",
